/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#sec-2">Results</a></li><li><a aria-current="false" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#sec-3">Discussion</a></li><li class="bordered"><a aria-current="false" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#backnotes">Notes</a></li><li><a aria-current="false" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#supplementary-materials">Supplementary Material</a></li><li><a aria-current="false" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#bibliography">References</a></li></ul></nav></div><nav id="article_collateral_menu"><ul class="collateral-pill" data-core-nav="collateral" style="margin-top: 0px;"><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-info" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="Information &amp; Authors" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-info" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-info"></i><span class="sr-only">Information &amp; Authors</span></a></li><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-metrics" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="Metrics &amp; Citations" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-metrics" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-timeline"></i><span class="sr-only">Metrics &amp; Citations</span></a></li><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-fulltext-options" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="View Options" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-fulltext-options" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-eye"></i><span class="sr-only">View Options</span></a></li><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-references" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="References" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-references" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-references"></i><span class="sr-only">References</span></a></li><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-media" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="Media" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-media" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-photo"></i><span class="sr-only">Media</span></a></li><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-tables" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="Tables" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-tables" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-tables"></i><span class="sr-only">Tables</span></a></li><li><a aria-controls="core-collateral-share" aria-expanded="false" data-offset="4px, 16px" data-original-title="Share" data-placement="left" data-toggle="tooltip" href="https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2110275#core-collateral-share" title=""><i aria-hidden="true" class="icon-share"></i><span class="sr-only">Share</span></a></li></ul></nav></div></div><div data-core-wrapper="content"><div data-extent="frontmatter" data-location="articleTab_article" id="abstracts"><div class="core-container"><section id="summary-abstract" property="abstract" role="doc-abstract" typeof="Text"><h2 property="name">Abstract</h2><section id="abs-sec-1"><h3>Background</h3><div role="paragraph">Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) is a major cause of lower respiratory tract infection and hospitalization in infants. Nirsevimab is a monoclonal antibody to the RSV fusion protein that has an extended half-life. The efficacy and safety of nirsevimab in healthy late-preterm and term infants are uncertain.</div></section><section id="abs-sec-2"><h3>Methods</h3><div role="paragraph">We randomly assigned, in a 2:1 ratio, infants who had been born at a gestational age of at least 35 weeks to receive a single intramuscular injection of nirsevimab or placebo before the start of an RSV season. The primary efficacy end point was medically attended RSV-associated lower respiratory tract infection through 150 days after the injection. The secondary efficacy end point was hospitalization for RSV-associated lower respiratory tract infection through 150 days after the injection.<div data-style-type="float-right"><div class="core-digital-object nejm-research-summary">
